---
import InfoLayout from "../layouts/InfoLayout.astro";
import { CONTACT_RETENTION_DAYS } from "../lib/contact/store";
import { AVATAR_MAX_BYTES } from "../lib/profile/avatar";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../lib/site";

const sections = [
  { slug: "accounts", text: "Accounts" },
  { slug: "data", text: "Your data" },
  { slug: "tools", text: "Tools" },
  { slug: "games", text: "Games" },
  { slug: "minecraft", text: "Minecraft" },
  { slug: "study", text: "Study" },
  { slug: "wiki", text: "The wiki" },
  { slug: "comments", text: "Comments" },
  { slug: "forum", text: "The forum" },
  { slug: "site", text: "The site" },
] as const;
import { DEFAULT_SETTINGS } from "@trilleo/study";
---

<InfoLayout path="/faq/" sections={sections}>
  <h2 id="accounts">Accounts</h2>
  <h3>Do I need an account?</h3>
  <p>
    No. Reading, the tools and the games all work without one. An account lets
    you comment, post in the forum, keep tool data and game saves across
    devices, and appear on leaderboards.
  </p>
  <h3>How do I sign in?</h3>
  <p>
    With your email address: type it on the
    <a href="/sign-in">sign-in page</a> and the site emails you a code. There’s
    no password, so there’s none to forget or leak. A new address makes an
    account the same way. You can also sign in with GitHub, and link GitHub to
    an account you made by email from your{" "}
    <a href="/account/security/">security page</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>I used to sign in with GitHub. What changed?</h3>
  <p>
    Nothing you need to do much about. Your account, username and everything in
    it stay; signing in with GitHub still works. Accounts are now known by their
    email address, so the first time you sign in, your account takes the address
    GitHub has verified for you (or asks for one). Renaming yourself on GitHub
    no longer changes anything here.
  </p>
  <h3>What’s a passkey?</h3>
  <p>
    A way to sign in with your fingerprint, face or screen lock instead of an
    email code. Add one on your <a href="/account/security/">security page</a>;
    your device or password manager keeps it, and the site only keeps its public
    half.
  </p>
  <h3>Will I know if someone else signs in?</h3>
  <p>
    If your account is signed in to from a browser it hasn’t used lately, you
    get an email (you can switch these off, though it’s better not to). Your
    security page also lists recent sign-ins. And if your address is changed,
    the old one gets a link that undoes it.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I change my username?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes, on your <a href="/account/profile/">profile page</a>, once a month.
    Links to your old username keep working for a while.
  </p>
  <h3>How old do I need to be?</h3>
  <p>At least 14 to make an account. Anyone can use the site signed out.</p>
  <h3>How do I delete my account?</h3>
  <p>
    From <a href="/account/">your account</a>: “Delete account” removes your
    profile, comments, tool data, game saves, files, Minecraft projects and
    email address. Your wiki edits stay in their pages’ history, and your forum
    posts in their topics, without your name; delete any posts you want gone
    first. Download your data first if you want to keep it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="data">Your data</h2>
  <h3>What do you know about me?</h3>
  <p>
    If you’ve never signed in: nothing. If you have: your{" "}
    <a href="/account/export.json">data export</a> lists all of it. The{" "}
    <a href="/legal/privacy/">privacy policy</a> explains each part.
  </p>
  <h3>Is there tracking or analytics?</h3>
  <p>
    No tracking and no ads. If you allow it, page views are counted by
    Cloudflare Web Analytics, which uses no cookies and doesn’t identify you;
    otherwise it doesn’t load at all. The site’s own cookies only keep you
    signed in. Change your choices any time with “Cookie settings” at the bottom
    of the page, or see <a href="/legal/cookies/">cookies</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>What happens to a message I send through the contact form?</h3>
  <p>
    Only I read it, and it’s deleted within {CONTACT_RETENTION_DAYS} days. If
    you leave an email address, it’s used only to reply.
  </p>
  <h3>Will the site email me?</h3>
  <p>
    Only if you add an address on your{" "}
    <a href="/account/email/">account’s email page</a> and confirm it with the
    code it sends. Then you get the notifications you leave on (replies to your
    comments, decisions about your uploads and projects), never newsletters.
    Every email has an unsubscribe link, and you can remove the address at any
    time.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tools">Tools</h2>
  <h3>Are my files uploaded when I convert them?</h3>
  <p>
    No. Convert, File info, Preview, QR and Color run entirely in your browser;
    the files never leave your device. That’s also why some formats depend on
    what your browser supports.
  </p>
  <h3>Why does Preview say a link was fetched through the server?</h3>
  <p>
    Browsers may only read a file from another site if that site allows it, and
    many don’t. Then Preview asks this site’s server to fetch it for you, which
    passes the file straight on and keeps nothing. The server is in mainland
    China, so links to services blocked there (Google Drive, Dropbox, OneDrive
    and others) only work if the site allows browsers in; otherwise download the
    file and drop it on the page.
  </p>
  <h3>Why do some Word or PowerPoint files look different in Preview?</h3>
  <p>
    Office files are laid out in your browser by small open-source readers, not
    by Office. Word documents come close; PowerPoint slides show their text,
    pictures and tables roughly where they sit. For a public link, Preview can
    open Microsoft’s own viewer instead, after telling you the link goes to
    Microsoft.
  </p>
  <h3>Where are my notes kept?</h3>
  <p>
    Signed out, in this browser only. Signed in, in your account, so they follow
    you. When you first sign in, Notes offers to move what’s in your browser.
  </p>

  <h2 id="games">Games</h2>
  <h3>Can I lose my Skygrid island?</h3>
  <p>
    Signed out, it lives in your browser: clearing site data deletes it. Sign in
    and it’s saved to your account, checked by the server as you play.
  </p>
  <h3>How does Hotfix know my score is real?</h3>
  <p>
    Signed in, each run starts from a course the server picked, and when it ends
    your browser sends the keys you pressed. The server plays them back through
    the same game and counts whatever that comes to. Records from playing signed
    out stay in your browser and never reach the leaderboards.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I sell coins or items?</h3>
  <p>
    Only in the game’s Bazaar, for other in-game things. Trading them for real
    money isn’t allowed (see the <a href="/legal/terms/#games">terms</a>).
  </p>
  <h3>I found a bug or an exploit.</h3>
  <p>
    Thank you: please <a href="/contact/">report it</a> instead of using it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="minecraft">Minecraft</h2>
  <h3>How do I share a creation?</h3>
  <p>
    Sign in, open <a href="/account/minecraft/">your projects</a> and make a new
    one: a page with pictures, then a release with its file. Drop the file in
    and most of the form fills itself in. Others see it once the release is
    published.
  </p>
  <h3>Why is my mod still waiting?</h3>
  <p>
    Mods and plugins run code on people’s computers, so every one is looked at
    by a person first. Other files wait only until you’re trusted (see the{" "}
    <a href="/legal/guidelines/#uploads">guidelines</a>).
  </p>
  <h3>Is this an official Minecraft site?</h3>
  <p>
    No. {SITE_NAME} is not an official Minecraft product, and is not approved by
    or associated with Mojang or Microsoft.
  </p>

  <h2 id="study">Study</h2>
  <h3>Where do the lessons come from?</h3>
  <p>
    From the materials of real classes, shared with their teachers’ permission;
    each subject names its teacher and course. The lessons are written from
    them: explanations, worked examples, formula sheets, quizzes and flashcards.
  </p>
  <h3>Does it keep my answers and reviews?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes, so it can schedule your flashcards and keep your mistake log. Signed
    out, they stay in this browser (or only this tab, if you turned preferences
    off in the cookie settings). Signed in, they go to your account and follow
    you; the dashboard offers to move what this browser has. They’re in your
    account export, and deleting your account deletes them.
  </p>
  <h3>How are reviews scheduled?</h3>
  <p>
    With FSRS, which works out from your own grades when you’re about to forget
    each card. A lesson’s flashcards join your reviews when you finish it, up to{" "}
    {DEFAULT_SETTINGS.newPerDay} new ones a day; you can change that and the
    recall it aims for on the <a href="/study/">study dashboard</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>I found a mistake in a lesson.</h3>
  <p>
    Please <a href="/contact/">say where</a>, and it will be fixed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="wiki">The wiki</h2>
  <h3>Who can edit the wiki?</h3>
  <p>
    Each wiki on <a href="https://wiki.trilleo.net/">wiki.trilleo.net</a>{" "}
    belongs to one project, and its owners choose its editors. Anyone can read a
    public wiki and see its pages’ source. Signed in, anyone can propose a
    change: the page’s “Propose a change” tab sends it to the wiki’s members,
    who approve or decline it, and you get an email either way. To edit
    directly, ask the wiki’s owner to add you.
  </p>
  <h3>How do I follow changes to a page?</h3>
  <p>
    Use “Watch this page” at the foot of a page, or “Watch this wiki” on its
    Recent changes; pages you edit or propose changes to are watched for you.
    Your watchlist (linked in every wiki’s sidebar) shows what others changed,
    and once a day at most a digest email sums it up. Switch the digest off on
    your <a href="/account/email/">email page</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>Why can’t a page be edited?</h3>
  <p>
    Some pages are generated from a project’s own data, such as Skygrid’s items
    and recipes, so they’re always up to date; they say so at the top. Others
    are protected, and only the wiki’s owners can change them.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I start a wiki for my server or project?</h3>
  <p>
    Wikis are started by the site’s admin: <a href="/contact/">get in touch</a>{" "}
    and say what it’s for.
  </p>

  <h2 id="comments">Comments</h2>
  <h3>Why hasn’t my comment appeared?</h3>
  <p>
    Your first comment waits until it’s read. After one is approved, the rest
    appear straight away. You can see your waiting comments under the post.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I comment under a different name?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes: set a display name in <a href="/account/profile/">your profile</a>, or
    choose to show only your username.
  </p>

  <h3>Can I have a profile picture?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes: add one on <a href="/account/profile/#avatar">your profile page</a>.
    PNG, JPEG or WebP up to {AVATAR_MAX_BYTES / 1024 / 1024} MB; your browser
    crops it to a square. It shows next to your name on comments, forum posts
    and your profile. Without one, your initials show instead.
  </p>

  <h2 id="forum">The forum</h2>
  <h3>Why hasn’t my topic appeared?</h3>
  <p>
    In most forums a newcomer’s first post waits until a moderator reads it;
    after one of your posts or comments is approved, the rest appear straight
    away. You can see your waiting posts in their forum, marked “Awaiting
    approval”. Some forums (like Announcements) are only for moderators.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I edit or delete what I posted?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes, with the Edit and Delete links under your post, while the topic is
    open. Editing is open for a while after posting (an hour for newcomers, a
    day for members, unless the admin changes it); every edit is kept, and
    moderators can see earlier versions. You can delete a topic you started
    until someone else replies.
  </p>
  <h3>How do I hear about replies?</h3>
  <p>
    You watch topics you start and reply to, and can watch any topic or forum
    with its Watch button. Replies, new topics, mentions, quotes, reactions and
    accepted answers show on your{" "}
    <a href="/account/notifications/">notifications page</a> (the account menu
    shows how many are new). On your{" "}
    <a href="/account/forum/">forum settings</a> choose whether they’re emailed
    at once, in a daily or weekly digest, or never.
  </p>
  <h3>What are points, ranks and badges?</h3>
  <p>
    Points come from your published posts, reactions others give them, and
    accepted answers. Your rank follows your points, and badges mark milestones.
    They show beside your posts and on your forum page.
  </p>
  <h3>What’s the difference between forum layouts?</h3>
  <p>
    Most forums show posts in order. In question-and-answer forums, answers are
    voted on and the asker accepts one, which comes first. In threaded forums,
    “Reply” under a post puts your answer beneath it.
  </p>
  <h3>Is what I’m writing saved?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes, with JavaScript on: a draft is saved to your account as you type, and
    offered back when you come back to that forum or topic. Posting clears it.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I add a poll or attach files?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes: “Add a poll” on the new-topic form, and the attachments box under any
    post (pictures, PDF, ZIP, text and Minecraft files, up to 5 per post).
    Attaching needs JavaScript. A newcomer’s files wait for review.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I message someone privately?</h3>
  <p>
    Once one of your posts or comments has been approved, yes: “Message” on a
    post or someone’s forum page, or your{" "}
    <a href="/account/messages/">messages page</a>. Choose who can message you
    on your <a href="/account/forum/">forum settings</a>, and block anyone.
    Messages are private; the admin sees one only if someone reports it.
  </p>
  <h3>How do I report a post or a picture?</h3>
  <p>
    “Report” under a post, or “Report picture” on someone’s profile, sends it to
    the forum’s moderators with a reason. A post reported by several established
    members is hidden until a moderator looks.
  </p>
  <h3>Why can’t I post?</h3>
  <p>
    A moderator may have banned you from posting for a while; the forum says
    why, and until when, when you try. You can still read it. Some forums are
    also only for certain groups.
  </p>
  <h3>How do I quote someone or mention them?</h3>
  <p>
    “Quote” under a post starts your reply with it. Typing @username links to
    that person’s profile, if it’s public.
  </p>

  <h2 id="site">The site</h2>
  <h3>Who runs {SITE_NAME}?</h3>
  <p>
    Trilleo, one person. See <a href="/about/">About</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>How is it built?</h3>
  <p>
    Astro, React and Postgres on a small server; the{" "}
    <a href="/colophon/">colophon</a> has the details and credits.
  </p>
  <h3>Is there a feed?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes: <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a>, with every post in full.
  </p>
  <h3>My question isn’t here.</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="/contact/">Ask it</a>.
  </p>
</InfoLayout>
